.TH LIBUMEM 3 "2026-10" "libumem" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
libumem \- userspace slab memory allocator with debugging facilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <umem.h>
.PP
.B cc
.I file.c
.B \-lumem
.fi
.PP
Or, without rebuilding the program:
.PP
.RS
.EX
LD_PRELOAD=\fIPREFIX\fR/lib/libumem_malloc.so ./myprog
.EE
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libumem
is the userspace slab allocator first shipped in Solaris 9, now the default
allocator on Solaris and illumos, ported to Linux, Windows and BSD-family
systems.  It provides the usual allocation entry points, an object-cache
interface for fixed-size objects, and \(em the reason most people reach for
it \(em facilities for finding memory bugs in a running program.
.PP
This page is a map.  Each area below has its own page.
.SS Allocating
.BR umem_alloc (3)
covers
.BR umem_alloc (),
.BR umem_zalloc (),
.BR umem_free ()
and the
.B UMEM_
flags.
.BR umem_cache_create (3)
covers the object-cache interface for fixed-size objects with optional
constructors and destructors.
.BR umem_hooks (3)
covers the allocation hooks.
.SS Debugging
.BR umem_debug (3)
is the page to read when hunting a bug.  It documents the
.B UMEM_DEBUG
and
.B UMEM_OPTIONS
environment variables and what each mode costs.  A sketch:
.PP
.RS
.TP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=default
audit + contents + guards; the usual choice
.TP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=audit
record an allocation stack per buffer
.TP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=guards
redzones either side of each buffer
.TP
.BI UMEM_DEBUG=firewall= N
VM-backed guard pages for buffers over
.I N
bytes
.TP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=lite
cheap subset, usable in production
.TP
.B UMEM_OPTIONS=tcache=0
disable the per-thread cache
.RE
.PP
Two things catch people out.
.B UMEM_DEBUG
is read during allocator initialisation, so it must be set
.I before
the process starts \(em exporting it later does nothing.  And when hunting a
.I leak,
set
.B UMEM_OPTIONS=tcache=0
as well: with the per-thread cache enabled a freed buffer can still be held
in a thread-local magazine, where it is reported as
.B CACHED
rather than freed, which makes leak output ambiguous.
.SS Inspecting a live process
.BR umem (1)
is the command-line tool:
.B umem --pid
.I PID
.BR status ,
.BR findleaks ,
.BR walk ,
.BR whatis ,
.BR bufctl ,
.BR log ,
.BR snapshot .
.BR umem_inspect (3)
documents the programmatic interface behind it.
.PP
The same commands are available inside a debugger.  See
.BR umem (1),
section FILES, for the installed locations of the GDB and LLDB modules and
how to load them; in short:
.PP
.RS
.EX
gdb  \-p \fIPID\fR \-ex 'source \fIPREFIX\fR/share/umem/debugger/gdb/umem_gdb.py'
lldb \-p \fIPID\fR \-o  'command script import \fIPREFIX\fR/share/umem/debugger/lldb/umem_lldb.py'
.EE
.RE
.PP
.BR umem_debugging (7)
is the longer narrative treatment of the whole debugging story.
.SH FILES
.I PREFIX
is the configure prefix
.RI ( /usr/local
by default).  On a Nix or Homebrew install it is the package's own
store/cellar directory, so resolve it with
.B pkg\-config \-\-variable=prefix libumem
rather than assuming.
.TP
.I PREFIX/lib/libumem.so
The allocator, for programs that link
.B \-lumem
directly.
.TP
.I PREFIX/lib/libumem_malloc.so
.BR malloc (3)
interposer, for
.B LD_PRELOAD
use against a program that was not built for libumem.
.TP
.I PREFIX/lib/pkgconfig/libumem.pc
pkg-config metadata.  Also the reliable way to find
.I PREFIX
itself.
.PP
See
.BR umem (1),
section FILES, for the tools and debugger modules.
.SH NOTES
Do not
.B LD_PRELOAD
the interposer into a language runtime you care about \(em Node, Python and
Electron have all been seen to crash under an interposed debug allocator.
Preload it into the program under investigation, not into your toolchain.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umem (1),
.BR umem_alloc (3),
.BR umem_cache_create (3),
.BR umem_debug (3),
.BR umem_hooks (3),
.BR umem_inspect (3),
.BR umem_debugging (7),
.BR gdb (1),
.BR lldb (1),
.BR malloc (3)
